edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186205018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1045 "Newton's Method for Optimizing Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186204834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204160 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c995ed3a-16cf-5c25-bab6-e3e845fcf5d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204510 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-2x-{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1.25$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d38c1ba4-f0b4-54be-934d-689905d21c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204510 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x-{y}^{2}+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(1) & 2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(1)-2 \\\\ (1)^{2}-2(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y-4x+2{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $10$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-6$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc5aadd2-f405-553a-a7ce-055d57e6d683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204512 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}y-4x+2{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}y-4x+2{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10y & 10x \\\\ 10x & 12y-12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}10y & 10x \\\\ 10x & 12y-12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}10y & 10x \\\\ 10x & 12y-12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}10xy-4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10(1) & 10(1) \\\\ 10(1) & 12(1)-12\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}10(1)(1)-4 \\\\ 5(1)^{2}+6(1)^{2}-12(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186204834 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204834 #edb/ref 1061 221904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204834 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94aaea32-ecd2-5fe5-9fc1-b64ab4029f1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204834 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204905 #edb/ref 1061 230728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd88cb98-b04e-57a7-8aac-a692591f907d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186204024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204906 #edb/ref 1061 230729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e6be020-bc4c-5c2a-8df7-29a3ad0e6e02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186204025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186205017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204907 #edb/ref 1061 230730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df49b891-cb31-5551-8e04-9950a2f7c37d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204908 #edb/ref 1061 230731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b52140a0-ea11-51ff-a2df-9341b9db3d41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186204026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205017 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205017 #edb/ref 1061 452234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205017 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82f60192-7f77-5ad5-b831-6e279defe546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205017 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205018 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205018 #edb/ref 1061 452235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205018 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "447d88c9-20f1-5f34-8959-ff06d8233d12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205018 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205133 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how Newton’s method can be used to find local minima or maxima of a smooth function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by approximating the function near the current guess with a parabola.\n\nRecall that in one variable, Newton’s update rule is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x)$ is the first derivative, and $f''(x)$ is the second derivative.\n\nTo generalize this idea to higher dimensions, we need analogues of the first and second derivatives.\n\n Recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x})$ generalizes the first derivative to multivariable functions, and the Hessian matrix $H_f(\\mathbf{x})$ generalizes the second derivative.\n\nLike before, we aim to find critical points, which are the points where the gradient vanishes:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo once again, optimization becomes a root-finding problem, but now for the gradient vector $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}).$\n\nJust like in one dimension, we approximate $f(\\mathbf{x})$ near our current guess $\\mathbf{x}_n$ using a second-order Taylor expansion\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{x}_n) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)^T (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n) + \\frac{1}{2} (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n)^T H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n) (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nThis is the multivariable analogue of a parabola, a *quadratic surface*.\n\nTo find the minimum of this quadratic surface, we compute its gradient with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ and set it equal to zero. The gradient of the approximation is\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n) + H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nWe then set the gradient equal to the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n) + H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}({x}_{n})(x-{x}_{n}) & =-∇f({x}_{n}) \\\\ x-{x}_{n} & =-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}) \\\\ x & ={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new value becomes our next guess, which we call $\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}.$ So, we write\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n).}\n$$\n\nNewton’s method can be extremely fast, but only under the following conditions:\n\n- The function $f$ is twice continuously differentiable (i.e., smooth).\n\n- The starting point $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is close to a critical point.\n\n- The Hessian $H_f(\\mathbf{x})$ is invertible and preferably positive definite near the solution.\n\nWhen these conditions are met, Newton’s method often converges in just a few iterations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205133 #edb/ref 1065 10739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205133 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81a83620-3aeb-50ae-93f0-c72ca9482763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205133 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205158 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the standard Newton’s method update for multivariable optimization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn higher dimensions, it’s often more useful to avoid calculating the inverse of a large Hessian matrix by rewriting the update as a linear system instead.\n\nLet’s see how.\n\nWe start with the original update formula:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $\\mathbf{x}_n$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n = -[H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides by $H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n){:}$\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nThis is a linear system of equations. It’s mathematically equivalent to the original update rule, but much better for actual computation, since we no longer need to invert the Hessian; we just need to solve for $(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205158 #edb/ref 1065 10940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205158 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2d1525f2-bf49-5f3e-859b-4aba4104f35c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205158 #edb/ref 1067 "Alternative to Newton's Method for Multivariable Functions Using Linear Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205203 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall the example we worked on, where we computed one iteration of the Newton–Raphson method. \n\nWe aim to find a critical point of the function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) =x^2y-2xy^2+x^3-4x,\n$$\n\nin the region $[0,2]\\times[0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$.\n\nNow, let’s carry out one iteration of Newton–Raphson using linear systems.\n\nThe Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+6(1) & 2(1)-4(1) \\\\ 2(1)-4(1) & -4(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(1)-2(1)^{2}+3(1)^{2}-4 \\\\ (1)^{2}-4(1)(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $\\begin{bmatrix}-1/18 \\\\ -13/18\\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{18} \\\\ \\frac{5}{18}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the same result we obtained previously." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186205203 #edb/ref 1065 13152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205203 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eb0b2e3d-6c4d-5e04-acc7-2f23e216e3e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205203 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205204 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times[0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =x^2y-2xy^2+x^3-4x.\n$$\n\nLet’s calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson method, starting at the point $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T.$ \n\nThe Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2y+6x & 2x-4y \\\\ 2x-4y & -4x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-2{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+6(1) & 2(1)-4(1) \\\\ 2(1)-4(1) & -4(1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(1)-2(1)^{2}+3(1)^{2}-4 \\\\ (1)^{2}-4(1)(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(8)\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{-36}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{18} \\\\ \\frac{5}{18}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186205204 #edb/ref 1065 13153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205204 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "42a507e6-8e0d-5b36-a901-6612002dd2b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205204 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290668 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290668 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290668 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0741d906-3cf0-5162-b5c9-1ea32e15c8ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290934 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290934 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290934 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "492c00c4-3107-5608-b527-e8bc5e7d7deb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291411 #edb/ref 1080 "0.75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b28405a2-0661-5ac2-aa4a-3de92efc267d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291505 #edb/ref 1080 "0.083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291505 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291505 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cdd8396b-9c0d-5787-83dd-edb8aa6ef559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291507 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291507 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291507 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ce4c51cc-3258-5a00-8acf-36973f30